A results table in the Thistle Dispatch console that broke the page on a phone.

```css
/* Before: the table is wider than the screen, so the whole page scrolls sideways. */
.results table { width: 900px; }
```

The symptom is a page that slides left and right, with the header and the navigation moving along with it.

```css
/* After: the wide thing scrolls inside its own box; the page does not. */
.results {
  overflow-x: auto;          /* the table scrolls here, not the document */
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
}
.results table {
  min-width: 45rem;          /* still readable; no longer pinned to the viewport */
  width: 100%;
}
.results td.reference {
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;   /* long tracking codes wrap instead of stretching a column */
}
```

And the layout around it, adapting with no breakpoint at all:

```css
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}
.card {
  min-width: 0;   /* without this, a long unbroken word can force the column wider */
}
```

One column at 360px, four on a laptop, and no media query was written. The `min-width: 0` is the line people omit and then spend an afternoon on: grid and flex children default to `min-width: auto`, which means "never shrink below your content", and one long identifier is enough to push the whole grid past the viewport.
